I’ve read in several places that I can set some $_SERVER data in the command line using something like this: misc="hahaha" php ./test.php When I test it with the following PHP, it works: <?php print "misc: {$_SERVER['misc']}
"; I want to override the $_SERVER[SCRIPT_NAME] and $_SERVER[SCRIPT_FILENAME] values. When I try: misc="hahaha" SCRIPT_NAME="/something.php" php ./test.php with: <?php print "SCRIPT_NAME: {$_SERVER['SCRIPT_NAME']}
"; print "SCRIPT_FILENAME: {$_SERVER['SCRIPT_FILENAME']}
"; print "misc: {$_SERVER['misc']}
"; the $_SERVER[SCRIPT_NAME] and $_SERVER[SCRIPT_FILENAME] values are for the actual script, and not my new value. I understand that I’m attempting to lie to PHP about the script it’s running, and so it’s a little cagey about this sort of thing. I want to do this because I am running a script which also runs from a web server, and I use those values to determine the web root: $root = str_replace($_SERVER['SCRIPT_NAME'], '', $_SERVER['SCRIPT_FILENAME']); Is there a way to set these values?
